<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juqu Anzhou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 460</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juqu Anzhou was a prince of the Northern Liang, brother of its last ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mujian">Juqu Mujian</a>. After Northern Wei conquered Northern Liang in 439, he and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-wuhui">Juqu Wuhui</a> fled west and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> in 442. Around 450 Anzhou destroyed the kingdom of Jushi, and he ruled Gaochang until the Rouran killed him in 460.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The date of Juqu Anzhou's birth is not known. He first appears in 431, when his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a> sent him on a visit to the Northern Wei capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a>, in modern Datong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>. When Northern Wei forces took the Northern Liang capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a> in 439 and seized his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mujian">Juqu Mujian</a>, Anzhou was governor of Ledu Commandery; he abandoned it and fled to Tuyuhun.</p><p>By the winter of 441 he had joined his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-wuhui">Juqu Wuhui</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>. Wuhui, who had just lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a> to Northern Wei, hoped to re-establish his rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a> and sent Anzhou against Shanshan, which at first held out. In 442 Wuhui came to join him, the king of Shanshan fled in fear, and the brothers took the city before moving later that year to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Anzhou succeeded Wuhui on his death in 444, and late that year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-liu-song">Emperor Wen of Liu Song</a> formally created him Prince of Hexi, the title his brothers and their father had used. Little is known of his reign at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>. He kept Wuhui's era name of Chengping, and after he took over the forces commanded by Wuhui's son Juqu Ganshou, Ganshou surrendered to Northern Wei. Anzhou appears to have sought good relations with Rouran.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 460, for reasons that are not known, Rouran attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>, killed Anzhou and then his clan, and made Kan Bozhou King of Gaochang in his place. Chinese historians dispute whether Wuhui and Anzhou should be counted as rulers of the Northern Liang at all, and most regard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mujian">Juqu Mujian</a> as its final prince.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juqu Anzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jushi survived as a vassal of successive Chinese and steppe powers until around 450, when <b>Juqu Anzhou</b>, leader of the Northern Liang remnants who had seized Gaochang, destroyed it.</blockquote><a href="/state/jushi">Jushi</a></li><li><blockquote>His brothers Juqu Wuhui and <b>Juqu Anzhou</b> fled west and ruled Gaochang from 442 until the Rouran Khaganate killed Anzhou in 460.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-liang">Northern Liang</a></li></ul></section>
